#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634576 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634556 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd88a3e9-cfa1-5b3f-b1c5-6c1939e13dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Canonical and Cross Product Form for a Given Straight Line"}] :question/id #uuid "2739d633-c98c-511d-b29b-7a186a02ea79" :question/math-academy-id "e-4916" :question/problem "The canonical equation of a line is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{-2}=\\dfrac{z+4}{5}.\n$$\n\nFind a vector equation of this line in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the denominators of the canonical equation, we find that the direction vector is ![](images/8c/8c0256434a5c5a1d28fc4ee3b1718cf9dc5561426e68c6f811462c7b5c53da63.svg) \n\nWe also have the position vector ![](images/06/0613af9addd1c1f2670a9dacdf65d69145732aeb67a9296fdbbfb08faf57fdea.svg) for the point $P(3,2,-4)$ that lies on the line. \n\nTherefore, the equation can be written as\n![](images/56/5663dd3a71842d52687f7ffc48ed1987d55534a34a4de19be821085690d4e738.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634599 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634549 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2393b236-cce0-5757-8e35-3d35cc7601ae" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Vector and Cross Product Form for a Given Straight Line"}] :question/id #uuid "45543759-b9e7-5e67-ac70-bc2614404f0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4918" :question/problem "Write the vector equation of the line\n![](images/50/506c32f1b035cfb996b4b7661aa40674e98028b49a20af03945301889ea5bb5c.svg)\nin the form $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p} + t\\mathbf{v},$ $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the line using the cross product takes the general form\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nComparing this general form to the given equation, we find that\n\n- the vector ![](images/d7/d70d0c3b77f98a6f111fff2a1f05ac97d1f37bb302638432598035e1c1d4c26d.svg) is parallel to the line, and\n\n- ![](images/48/4846eb5f6cce8f7e031226a6d66b450630d16823975da1f984a55c11499efe98.svg) is the position vector of the point $P(-7,3,8)$ that lies on the line.\n\nTherefore, the given vector equation can be written as\n![](images/30/30730fb92b509265709b0af22d81f9a6a1b7298b91aa01246bccc66462419028.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634640 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634552 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "714f4f34-4ef2-528e-9229-5ac73bddc038" :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Parametric and Cross Product Form for a Given Straight Line"}] :question/id #uuid "85e30267-bea2-5c0d-9a8b-db7f6fd16b0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4920" :question/problem "The parametric equations of a straight line are given by \n![](images/26/26e6fa3e3dc44d918debd54dd50e217ec0d7dd0ea64d2fc717a2d7da0ba370bb.svg)\nfor $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ Find the vector equation of the line in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p}) \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$" :question/worked-solution "The corresponding vector equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+4t \\\\ -5+2t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 2t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThe general form of the vector equation for a line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + \\mathbf{v}t,\n$$\n\nand comparing this general form to our equation above, we find that\n\n- ![](images/bf/bf30e73359f146289ca2aeccc9cdbd8b3aba681b19cc771b9d4e47b793afdbbe.svg) is the position vector of the point $P(2,-5,0)$ that lies on the line, and\n\n- the vector ![](images/32/32af2ee2cc81d7d2552887c17f130cc2873a856f3df8596076dae952e4a0a909.svg) is parallel to the line.\n\nTherefore, the cross product equation for the line is\n![](images/53/53b1e071da37371a873cf7628b9b910970adeba0e84c1af5a1f7bd19f0ebeb14.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634647 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634557 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fecaf27b-659c-59dd-887f-f154d5f83bd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cross Product Equation of a Line That Passes Through Two Points"}] :question/id #uuid "96216bf8-a1cf-5704-8edb-81c3fbdebb58" :question/math-academy-id "e-4922" :question/problem "A line passes through the points $P(4,-7,9)$ and $Q(3,-5,8).$ Find its vector equation in the form $\\mathbf{r} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{c}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(4,-7,9)$ and $Q(3,-5,8)$. Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, starting with the equation of the line using the cross product, we manipulate the equation and then substitute in our values of $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v-p\\times v & =0 \\\\ r\\times v & =p\\times v \\\\ r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =-11i-5j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
